<clickhouse>
    <!--
        Ingest settings (one file per concern; mounted as a single file because the image entrypoint
        writes generated user files into /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/).

        WHY
        `events` is PARTITION BY toYYYYMM(timestamp). The Kafka engine hands the materialized view one
        block per poll (up to kafka_max_block_size = 65536 messages), and ClickHouse refuses an INSERT
        block that touches more than max_partitions_per_insert_block partitions (default 100) with
        TOO_MANY_PARTS (Code 252, 'Too many partitions for single INSERT block'). The Kafka consumer then
        does not commit the offset and re-reads the same block forever, so ingestion stalls. Valid
        timestamps span 2000-01 .. now+366d (the EventNormalizer clamp), about 335 months and growing by
        1 per month, so one honest historical backfill (or any holder of a public project key) can
        trigger it.

        WHY 1000
        max_partitions_per_insert_block counts DISTINCT PARTITIONS IN ONE INSERT BLOCK. It is a different
        dimension from parts_to_delay_insert (1000) / parts_to_throw_insert (3000), which count ACTIVE
        PARTS INSIDE ONE PARTITION; do not compare the numbers. 1000 is well above the ~335 possible
        months. A hostile block adds at most 1 part per partition per poll, so merges would have to lag
        ~1000 polls (~2 h) to reach the delay and ~3000 polls (~6 h) to reach the throw. The only global
        bound is max_parts_in_total (100000), besides disk space.

        KEEP IN SYNC
        If EventNormalizer::MIN_TIMESTAMP / MAX_DAYS_AHEAD or the 2000 floor is widened, or the partition
        key of `events` changes, the month count above must be revisited together with the MV guards in
        001_events.sql.

        NOTE ON COMMANDS
        XML comments cannot contain two consecutive hyphens, so long options below are written with a
        space after the first hyphen: read '- -force-recreate' as two hyphens followed by force-recreate.

        HOW TO APPLY
        This is a SINGLE-FILE bind mount, which follows the inode. Editing the file in place (sed -i,
        most editors) or `git pull` / checkout (unlink + create) is NOT picked up by
        `docker compose up -d`. Run:
            docker compose up -d - -force-recreate clickhouse

        HOW TO RECOGNIZE THE WEDGE
            SELECT exceptions.text, last_commit_time, last_poll_time
            FROM system.kafka_consumers
            WHERE database = 'posthog' AND table = 'kafka_events'
        A stale last_commit_time with a fresh last_poll_time and a repeating exception means the consumer
        is stalled. Key on the exception MESSAGE ('Too many partitions for single INSERT block' vs
        'Too many parts (N)'), not only on Code 252. ANY persistent MV insert failure (disk full,
        per-partition too many parts, MV expression errors) wedges the Kafka consumer the same way.

        LAST RESORT (can LOSE data)
        Skip the stuck block by resetting the consumer group offsets, with the ClickHouse Kafka table
        detached or the server stopped:
            docker compose exec -T kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
                - -bootstrap-server localhost:9092 - -group clickhouse_events - -topic events \
                - -reset-offsets - -to-latest - -execute
        Alternatively detach / reattach the Kafka table.

        VERIFY
        `scripts/smoke-capture.sh - -with-wedge-check` checks the limit end to end.

        PROFILES
        The setting is read from the server's default profile for the Kafka engine's insert context (in
        this stack the `posthog` user also uses `default`). A read-only user defined later must use its
        OWN profile, named e.g. `ro_insights` (NOT `readonly`: the stock users config already defines a
        profile with that name).
    -->
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <max_partitions_per_insert_block>1000</max_partitions_per_insert_block>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
